# Exclude this board's RAUC A/B partitions (metadata1/2, fip-a/b,
# u-boot-env, bootfs-a/b, vendorfs-a/b, rootfs-a/b) from udev's generic
# auto-mounter.
#
# mount-partitions.sh already mounts whichever of these is the *active*
# slot (scoped to root's own device - see its get_type/found_devices
# functions), and RAUC needs exclusive access to the *inactive* slot to
# write an update into. The generic automounter doesn't know about any of
# that: it mounts every filesystem-bearing partition it sees under
# /run/media/<label>-<devnode>, active or not, which both duplicates
# mount-partitions.sh's job for the active slot and holds the inactive
# slot's device open - the direct cause of RAUC failing with "Device or
# resource busy" when installing an update.
#
# eMMC (mmcblk1) and SD card (mmcblk0) do NOT share the same partition
# numbering here, despite carrying the same partition names/content: the
# SD card's GPT carries two extra fsbla1/fsbla2 (FSBL) partitions at the
# front that eMMC doesn't need (eMMC stores its FSBL in dedicated
# mmcblk1boot0/boot1 hardware partitions instead), shifting every
# subsequent partition's number by 2. So on eMMC, partitions 1-11 are the
# A/B set and userfs is p12; on the SD card, partitions 1-13 are the A/B
# set (plus fsbla1/fsbla2) and userfs is p14. Confirmed live: an earlier
# version of this file used a combined `mmcblk[01]pN` range of 1-11 for
# both devices, which correctly covered eMMC but left the SD card's
# rootfs-a/rootfs-b (actually p12/p13 there, outside that range) exposed
# to the automounter - caught when they showed up auto-mounted under
# /run/media/ during a real SD card boot test. Each device therefore gets
# its own explicit range below rather than one shared pattern.
#
# userfs (the last partition on each device) is intentionally left
# mountable here; it's not part of the A/B scheme and isn't otherwise
# auto-mounted by anything else.
#
# expr match (used by mount.sh) matches a PREFIX of $DEVNAME against a BRE,
# so each pattern is anchored with $ to avoid e.g. "p1" prefix-matching
# "p10"/"p11".
/dev/mmcblk1p1$
/dev/mmcblk1p2$
/dev/mmcblk1p3$
/dev/mmcblk1p4$
/dev/mmcblk1p5$
/dev/mmcblk1p6$
/dev/mmcblk1p7$
/dev/mmcblk1p8$
/dev/mmcblk1p9$
/dev/mmcblk1p10$
/dev/mmcblk1p11$
/dev/mmcblk0p1$
/dev/mmcblk0p2$
/dev/mmcblk0p3$
/dev/mmcblk0p4$
/dev/mmcblk0p5$
/dev/mmcblk0p6$
/dev/mmcblk0p7$
/dev/mmcblk0p8$
/dev/mmcblk0p9$
/dev/mmcblk0p10$
/dev/mmcblk0p11$
/dev/mmcblk0p12$
/dev/mmcblk0p13$
